## Troubleshooting networking issues in Kubernetes clusters

This runbook exists to help outline the use of our Analytical Platform Network Diagnostics image when troubleshooting networking related issues in our Kubernetes clusters.

If you attempt to use the default ubuntu image you won't be able to use network tools or install them:

    ```

    ubuntu@hmcts-sdp-debug:/$ nc
    bash: nc: command not found

    ubuntu@hmcts-sdp-debug:/$ ping
    bash: ping: command not found

    ubuntu@hmcts-sdp-debug:/$ traceroute
    bash: traceroute: command not found

    ```


To solve this problem, we have created [an image with these tools installed](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform-network-diagnostics).


### Assumptions

- You are operating in the development container in the [analytical-platform repository](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform).
- This example is in the `airflow` namespace, troubleshoot in the appropriate namespace for the problem you are trying to solve.

### Consuming the image

1. Authenticate to the cluster

    ```
    kubectl get pods
    ```

1. Create the YAML file.

    ```

    ---
    apiVersion: v1
    kind: Pod
    metadata:
    name: network-debug
    namespace: mwaa
    spec:
    securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true
        runAsUser: 1000
        seccompProfile:
        type: RuntimeDefault
    containers:
        - name: ubuntu
        image: ghcr.io/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform-network-diagnostics:1.1.0
        imagePullPolicy: Always
        command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
        securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
            drop:
                - ALL
        resources:
            requests:
            memory: "256Mi"
            cpu: "250m"
            limits:
            memory: "512Mi"
            cpu: "500m"
    ```

    NOTE: Depending on the nature of the troubleshooting that you are doing, you may need to include a hostAliases block such as the below in your YAML file:

    ```

    spec:
    securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true
        runAsUser: 1000
    hostAliases:
        - ip: "10.168.5.13"
        hostnames:
            - "mipersistentprod.blob.core.windows.net"

    ```

1. Apply the YAML file.
Run the following command from the directory where your `pod.yml` file is located:

    ```
    kubectl apply -f pod.yml
    ```

1. Verify the pod is running.

    ```
    kubectl get pods -n mwaa
    ```

1. Exec to the container.

    ```
    kubectl exec -it network-debug -n mwaa -- /bin/bash
    ```

    Now you are in a container which has [the tools listed here](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform-network-diagnostics/blob/fac6afcebfe6dbf6b9e88ca81343ec9dd427dde9/Dockerfile#L36). Good luck troubleshooting!

1. Delete the pod when you have finished troubleshooting.

    ```
    kubectl delete pod network-debug -n mwaa
    ```
